))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425555} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c9a569-26b9-5e10-bb19-d368180a496e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425669} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29b13204-0781-5f66-8e20-c7b799f66b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425567} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd22fb5-30e1-5875-ad13-91c8a0b3a92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425622} {:answer/id #uuid "e698d1ad-201e-53de-8029-7a234688dc21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425629} {:answer/id #uuid "fa872a28-3d62-5bdb-817b-84e0219ef7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e698d1ad-201e-53de-8029-7a234688dc21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425629} :answer-field/id #uuid "e973ecec-8364-5900-9b48-eecc739a0ce6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9666624-6b3b-5d5b-bd5e-3f9ae5e53130" :question/math-academy-id "q-343337" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=6x$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $6$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=3{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n6x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-3,3).$ \nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}6t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ [3{t}^{2}]_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{3}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3}\\right)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069197c9-45d7-5029-a771-c125abb39812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425548} {:answer/id #uuid "2560f1d4-c701-5a15-a0c6-099ceec0a029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/re